WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.120
And so, for example, we typically,

2
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.240
as humans, mammals, have a free-running period.

3
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.750
If there was no environmental signals,

4
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.440
circadian rhythms would run

5
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.970
for a little over 24 hours, about 24.2 hours.

6
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.860
And so if you have strong environmental signals,

7
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.240
like you are outdoors in natural light exposure

8
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.010
all day, for example,

9
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.580
you will typically see that

10
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.530
with that strong strength of that time cue,

11
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:32.540
which is known in the literature

12
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.430
by the German word zeitgeber,

13
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:35.890
literally time giver,

14
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.470
that will typically result in circadian rhythms

15
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.060
synchronizing in an earlier phase

16
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.390
to the 24-hour day.

17
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.000
Whereas if we have weak strength of zeitgebers,

18
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.440
which we typically do

19
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.270
in Western industrialized countries,

20
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.210
we have urban environments, indoor lighting,

21
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.790
artificial light exposure,

22
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.280
this typically results in the phase

23
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.940
of the internal clock drifting later

24
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.450
rather than earlier.

25
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.480
Whereas, like I said,

26
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.160
when the environment emits strong time cues

27
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.810
such as natural light conditions,

28
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.760
the internal clock advances

29
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.400
by an average of about 30 minutes

30
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.250
per one hour of outdoor light exposure.

31
00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.950
Now, this is still chronotype dependent.

32
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.570
If you're a late chronotype,

33
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.800
that shifting forward is not you shifting

34
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.360
as forward as an early chronotype,

35
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.530
if that makes sense.

36
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.530
It's still chronotype dependent.

37
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.370
So if you fly from London to Sydney

38
00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.730
and you are massively jet-lagged

39
00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.910
for up to a week,

40
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.800
you don't, your chronotype doesn't change

41
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.120
when you have synchronized

42
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.510
and adapted to your new time zone.

43
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.590
You remain an evening chronotype, you remain.

44
00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.660
But the strength of those time cues

45
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.070
can influence the phase, shall we say,

46
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.810
of the internal clock timing.
